#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205291 :tutorial/content "When factoring a trinomial, we should always begin by factoring out any constant factors. Then, we factor whatever quadratic remains using the techniques we've studied. \n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n6y^2+21y+9\n$$\n\nThe first thing to notice is that all the terms have a common factor of $3.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3(2y^2 + 7y + 3)\n$$\n\nSo, our goal now is to factor the trinomial in parentheses:\n\n$$\n2y^2 + 7y + 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 3 = 6$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nAlso, since all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the *positive* factor pairs of $6.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6),\\quad (2,3).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,6)$ add to $7{:}$ \n\n$$\n1+6 =7  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $y+6y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}+7y+3= \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+y+6y+3 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}+y)+(6y+3) & = \\\\ y(2y+1)+3(2y+1) & = \\\\ (y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}+21y+9 & = \\\\ 3(2{y}^{2}+7y+3) & = \\\\ 3(y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWhenever the coefficient of the quadratic term is negative, we should always factor this out first. That way, we're left with the task of factoring a trinomial containing a positive quadratic coefficient. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "35b6c679-c9c6-5b44-8de5-21106bc70674" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9456 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
